# Reed-Gate Electroclash

**Ingredients:** HAUSA ALGAITA + ELECTROCLASH / MINIMAL NEW WAVE + KALANGU TALKING DRUM + PREPARED CLAVINET + BASS CLARINET + SHORT MONO ANALOG BASS

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a harmattan-season residency in a decommissioned short-wave transmitter workshop outside Kano, where algaita player Amina Garba was testing clipped ceremonial reed phrases in a dry repair room while Berlin producer Lukas Kern programmed skeletal electroclash patterns on a drum machine, mono synth, and battered step sequencer. The breakthrough came when Lukas stopped treating the algaita as a lead melody and listened instead to the tiny breath closures between phrases. Each closure created a hard acoustic gate: piercing reed tone, sudden vacuum, then another burst. He rebuilt the electronic groove around those vacancies. Synth bass, clap, prepared clavinet, and machine percussion were allowed to enter only when the reed released the air column. Kalangu talking drum and bass clarinet completed a form where breath itself divides the arrangement into open and closed electrical states.

Reed-Gate Electroclash is a dry, high-contrast acoustic-electronic dance form built from the penetrating double-reed attack of the Hausa algaita and the severe repetition of early electroclash and minimal new wave. It usually sits between 122 and 132 BPM in 4/4, using compact kick, clipped clap, sparse hats, narrow analog-bass pulses, deadpan sequencer figures, and abrupt one-beat silences. The algaita supplies the structural authority through breath bursts, fingered modal cells, lip-pressure inflection, overblown upper-register cries, and sudden complete cutoffs.

The algaita is recorded with one small-diaphragm condenser 40–60 cm off-axis from the bell, one ribbon microphone farther back for body and reduced harshness, and one restrained room microphone. The close microphone preserves finger articulation and reed edge without placing the capsule directly in the air blast. The ribbon supplies midrange weight. The room microphone is automated upward only during exposed endings and long held notes.

Players favor compact pentatonic and modal fragments, tonic-fifth anchors, flat-second tension, minor thirds, repeated neighbor tones, narrow ornamental turns, and two- to six-note calls. Continuous virtuosic lines are avoided. The genre depends on making the absence after each burst as audible as the burst itself.

The central compositional device is the **Reed Gate**. Every algaita phrase is classified as OPEN while air and pitch are present, then CLOSED the instant the player ends the breath column. Certain electronic events are forbidden during OPEN state. A clap, synth-bass note, clavinet stab, or kalangu response may enter only after the reed closes. The track therefore behaves like a circuit whose switching is controlled by a human breath phrase rather than a sidechain envelope.

A second device, **Pressure Threshold**, divides algaita attacks into SOFT, HARD, and OVERBLOWN states. SOFT attacks permit only light electronic replies. HARD attacks authorize kick-plus-bass punctuation. OVERBLOWN attacks trigger the most complete response: clap, bass, prepared clavinet, and one low acoustic answer. The mapping is compositional rather than sensor-driven; the performers learn it as a shared score.

A third device, **Vacuum Bar**, reserves one full bar in which the algaita may play but no electronic pitched instrument is allowed. Only dry drum-machine transients and kalangu may answer. The absence of synth harmony prevents the acoustic reed from becoming another layer in a dense retro arrangement.

A fourth device, **Leak Current**, allows one intentionally quiet analog-synth tone to survive underneath a CLOSED state, but it must disappear the instant the next algaita breath begins. This creates the inverse of conventional ducking: the electronic sustain exists only in the acoustic vacuum.

A fifth device, **Bell Shadow**, places bass clarinet or prepared clavinet on the final pitch class of an algaita phrase only after the reed has stopped. The supporting instrument therefore behaves like a delayed acoustic/electric afterimage rather than simultaneous harmony.

Electroclash drums remain blunt and economical. Kick is short, dry, and slightly boxy rather than sub-heavy. Clap or snare is clipped and often mono. Hats appear as sparse eighths or short sixteenth bursts. Occasional tom or metal-tick hits create machine severity without industrial-cinematic excess. Fills are usually one beat or less.

Short mono analog bass uses root, fifth, octave, flat seventh, and occasional chromatic approach notes with fast release. Bass is normally silent while the algaita is sustaining. It enters in the breath gap, making the low end feel like a reply rather than a floor. Slides are minimal and portamento is restricted to short transitions between phrase blocks.

Prepared clavinet supplies brittle upper-mid punctuation. Selected strings are damped with removable felt or paper. Parts favor open fifths, suspended seconds, minor sevenths, dead-note sixteenths, and clipped two-note dyads. The clavinet never doubles the full reed line. It answers the termination point.

Kalangu talking drum provides pressure-shaped acoustic rhythm through short squeezed pitch bends, dry stick attacks, rim taps, and compact two- or three-hit replies. Its role is conversational rather than decorative. Because the kalangu can change pitch continuously with arm pressure, it often mirrors the contour of the final algaita note after the reed has already stopped.

Bass clarinet provides low acoustic counterweight through chalumeau fundamentals, tongue stops, breath-only attacks, falling minor seconds, and dry key noise. It appears mainly during Vacuum Bars and Bell Shadow sections where synth bass is absent.

Vocals are optional and sparse. Hausa, German, or English fragments may reference carrier waves, transmitter lamps, breath checks, switch states, dust filters, or instructions such as “reed closed,” “current waits,” “hold the air,” and “after the bell.” Delivery should be spoken, clipped, deadpan, or rhythmically shouted. Conventional sung choruses are avoided.

Effects remain disciplined. Algaita receives a short room and minimal dark plate. Clavinet can use a tiny spring. Bass clarinet gets a compact chamber. Analog bass uses mild saturation. Drum-machine ambience is short and gated. No synth pad wash, shimmer, festival risers, long 808 glides, orchestral reed layers, or retro-futurist laser effects are allowed.

The defining tension is acoustic breath versus electrical persistence. The reed can dominate the room, but every time the player stops blowing, the machine gets permission to speak.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 122–132 BPM, primarily 4/4 electroclash / minimal-new-wave pulse with compact kick, clipped clap, sparse hats, short mono bass, and algaita phrases functioning as alternating OPEN/CLOSED structural states.

**Harmonic Palette:** Pentatonic and modal centers, tonic-fifth anchors, flat-second tension, minor thirds, suspended seconds, minor sevenths, octave reinforcement, and static one- or two-chord electronic fields that leave the reed exposed.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Reed Gate** — electronic bass, chord, or clap events may be restricted to the silence immediately after an algaita breath phrase.
2. **Pressure Threshold** — SOFT, HARD, and OVERBLOWN reed attacks authorize progressively denser electronic responses.
3. **Vacuum Bar** — one full bar removes electronic pitched material while algaita and dry rhythm remain active.
4. **Leak Current** — a quiet synth sustain exists only during the breath gap and is cut the moment the reed returns.
5. **Bell Shadow** — bass clarinet or clavinet repeats or reframes the final reed pitch only after the algaita stops.
6. **No-Sustained-Doubling Rule** — synths and winds cannot continuously double the algaita line; the acoustic reed must retain its own spectral territory.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through harder reed pressure, shorter breath gaps, more frequent OVERBLOWN thresholds, denser machine claps, stronger kalangu replies, and tighter bass punctuation. Climaxes remain dry, angular, and confrontational rather than huge.

# Scene & Origin Lore

Reed-Gate Electroclash formed inside a retired short-wave radio transmitter and repair complex outside Kano. The rooms retained ceramic insulators, copper bus bars, frequency charts, tube sockets, dust-coated oscilloscopes, ventilation louvers, signal lamps, paper maintenance logs, steel racks, and heavy doors lined with faded acoustic felt.

Algaita player **Amina Garba** had been using the former test room because its dry walls made phrase endings unusually obvious. She was interested in how strongly a short reed call could define silence after it stopped.

Berlin producer **Lukas Kern** arrived with a compact drum machine, mono bass synth, step sequencer, and several austere electroclash sketches. His first arrangement placed Amina over a continuous synth bass and sounded crowded. During a test, the bass channel failed for several seconds and returned exactly when Amina stopped playing. The accidental reply felt more decisive than the original arrangement. Lukas rewrote the bass so it could occur only after her breath closed.

Kalangu player **Bashir Lawal** joined after realizing the talking drum could answer not just the rhythm but the pressure contour of the reed's final pitch. Prepared-clavinet player **Marta Vogel** developed dry harmonic stabs that occupied only the post-breath gaps. Bass-clarinetist **Idris Nwosu** completed the group with low acoustic replies for sections where the synth disappeared entirely.

Their first performances were called **carrier tests**. A small panel behind the group displayed only two lamps: OPEN and CLOSED. The lamps were operated manually by a stage assistant following the score; they controlled no sound. Audience members gradually learned that the electronic band seemed to become active only when the reed lamp went dark.

The visual language developed around dust-red earth, transmitter green, oxidized copper, ceramic white, faded frequency-chart blue, black Bakelite, dull aluminum, and amber valve glow. The unofficial rule is that if the electronic arrangement can run unchanged while the algaita player rests, the composition has failed.

